  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.laplacian_spectrum - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.laplacian_spectrum.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.laplacian_spectrum()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.laplacian_spectrum()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.laplacian_spectrum()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L89299-L89448){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.laplacian_spectrum(\n    which=\"smallest\",\n    k=None,\n    normalized=False,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Laplacian spectrum](../assets/graphs/laplacian-spectrum.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A five-node graph, two triangles sharing an edge plus a pendant, and a bar chart of the five eigenvalues of its Laplacian D minus A, sorted from smallest to largest: 0.000, 0.830, 2.689, 4.000, and 4.481. The single zero means the graph is connected, and the second-smallest eigenvalue, the algebraic connectivity 0.830, is in the accent color.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nReturns the spectrum of the Laplacian matrix `L = D - A` (or normalized variant). The\nLaplacian spectrum provides insights into graph connectivity, cuts, and diffusion processes.\n\n`A` is the weighted adjacency matrix used by `adjacency_spectrum()` and `D` is the diagonal\nmatrix of its row sums (out-degrees for directed graphs), matching\n`networkx.laplacian_matrix()`. Parallel edges sum their weights and self-loops cancel out\nof `L`, so its rows always sum to zero.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[which]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;smallest&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Which eigenvalues to return: `\"all\"` for all `n` eigenvalues, `\"smallest\"` for the\n    smallest `k`, or `\"largest\"` for the largest `k`. For directed graphs, \"largest\" and\n    \"smallest\" are by real part.\n\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Number of eigenvalues to return for `\"largest\"`/`\"smallest\"`. Default is `min(6, n)`\n    where `n` is the number of nodes.\n\n<code>[normalized]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, use the normalized Laplacian `L_norm = D^(-1/2) L D^(-1/2)`, where isolated\n    nodes get a zero row and column (as in `networkx.normalized_laplacian_matrix()`). Its\n    eigenvalues lie in `[0, 2]` for undirected graphs.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Dictionary with keys:\n\n    - `\"values\"`: list of eigenvalues, sorted ascending\n    - `\"vectors\"`: list of unit eigenvectors aligned with `\"values\"`; entry `j` of each\n    vector belongs to node `nodes[j]`\n    - `\"nodes\"`: node IDs, in creation order, giving the order of eigenvector entries\n    - `\"algebraic_connectivity\"`: `λ₂` over the full spectrum (second-smallest eigenvalue;\n    `0` if disconnected, `None` for a single node)\n\n    For directed graphs the Laplacian is not symmetric, so `\"values\"` holds Python\n    `complex` numbers sorted by real part, and `\"vectors\"` and `\"algebraic_connectivity\"`\n    are `None`.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is empty, `which` is invalid, or the eigenvalue computation fails to\n    converge.\n:::\n\n## Common Use Cases {.doc-common-use-cases}\n\n- spectral clustering: use Fiedler vector for graph partitioning\n- community detection: k-way clustering using k eigenvectors\n- graph cuts: min-cut via spectral relaxation\n- synchronization: analyze coupled oscillators on networks\n- diffusion processes: heat kernel and random walks\n- network robustness: algebraic connectivity as connectivity measure\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe Laplacian $L = D - A$ carries the same structural information as the adjacency\nmatrix but organized so that its algebraic properties align with graph connectivity\nin particularly clean ways. $L$ is always positive semi-definite, so its spectrum is\nnon-negative, and it *always* has a zero eigenvalue whose eigenvector is the\nconstant vector $\\mathbf{1}$. The multiplicity of that zero eigenvalue is exactly\nthe number of connected components, so counting near-zero eigenvalues is the direct\nspectral test for disconnection. The second-smallest eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$, called\nthe *algebraic connectivity* or Fiedler value, is positive iff the graph is\nconnected and quantifies expansion through Cheeger's inequality\n$\\tfrac{h^2}{2\\Delta} \\le \\lambda_2 \\le 2h$, where $h$ is edge expansion. Its\neigenvector (the *Fiedler vector*) partitions the graph into two well-connected\nhalves by sign, minimizing normalized cut weight in the spectral-relaxation sense,\nand is the workhorse of spectral bisection algorithms.\n\nPassing `normalized=True` switches to\n$L_{\\mathrm{norm}} = D^{-1/2} L D^{-1/2}$, whose eigenvalues sit in $[0, 2]$\nregardless of degree distribution; a $\\lambda = 2$ eigenvalue signals a bipartite\ncomponent. This variant is standard in normalized-cut clustering because it handles\ndegree-imbalanced graphs (star-plus-clique structures, for instance) far more\ngracefully than the plain Laplacian. Signature spectra include\n$K_n \\to \\{0, n, \\ldots, n\\}$, the path\n$P_n \\to \\{2 - 2\\cos(\\pi k / n)\\}$, the cycle\n$C_n \\to \\{2 - 2\\cos(2\\pi k / n)\\}$, and the star\n$S_n \\to \\{0, 1, \\ldots, 1, n\\}$.\n\nFor undirected graphs with at least 400 nodes, `\"smallest\"` and `\"largest\"` with a\nsmall `k` use a sparse block solver (Chebyshev-filtered subspace iteration) that\nscales to very large graphs and resolves repeated eigenvalues, such as the many zero\neigenvalues of a disconnected graph. Results match the dense solver to about `1e-11`\nrelative to the spectral radius. Convergence depends on the requested eigenvalues'\ngap relative to the width of the spectrum. The smallest eigenvalues of the\n*unnormalized* Laplacian are the hard case on graphs with high-degree hubs, since\nhub degrees stretch the spectrum: on a 200,000-node Barabási-Albert graph they take\nminutes, while `normalized=True` takes seconds. `\"all\"`, small graphs, and directed\ngraphs use a dense $O(n^3)$ eigendecomposition, practical up to a few thousand nodes.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~laplacian_matrix`) \n\n:   The matrix whose eigenvalues these are.\n\n[](`~algebraic_connectivity`) \n\n:   The second smallest eigenvalue on its own.\n\n[](`~fiedler_vector`) \n\n:   The eigenvector of the second smallest eigenvalue.\n\n[](`~adjacency_spectrum`) \n\n:   The eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix instead.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThere are as many zero eigenvalues as connected components, two here:\n\n::: {#a0ebb515 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"D\", \"E\")])\nspectrum = graph.laplacian_spectrum(which=\"all\")\n\n[round(v, 3) + 0.0 for v in spectrum[\"values\"]]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Cutting the two clubs apart\n\n`friends()` links 24 members of two clubs in a made-up town, a chess club and a climbing\nclub, who are friends. With the five friendships between the clubs deleted, the\nspectrum has two zeros, one for each club. In the whole network the second smallest\neigenvalue, the algebraic connectivity, is small but no longer `0`:\n\n::: {#91d0b222 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\nclub = friends.get_node_attrs(\"club\")\napart = (\n    ec.friends()\n    .select_edges(lambda e: club[e.source] != club[e.target])\n    .delete_edges_ws()\n)\n\ndef smallest(graph):\n    values = graph.laplacian_spectrum(k=3)[\"values\"]\n    return [round(v, 3) + 0.0 for v in values]\n\nsmallest(apart), smallest(friends)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n([0.0, 0.0, 0.388], [0.0, 0.212, 0.513])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#6782fd85 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\napart.plot(color_by=\"club\", label_by=\"label\", seed=8)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"51.7 14.0 688.8 492.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"51.7\" y=\"14.0\" width=\"688.8\" height=\"492.0\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n